[38;5;252m[1m## Behaviour I checked by reading, not by testing[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three differences exist in principle but are unreachable in practice, so I left them alone:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- C computes [38;5;10mint l = strlen(src) + 1[0m; Rust uses [38;5;10musize[0m. These diverge only for strings near [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m, where the C behaviour is signed-overflow UB.[0m[0m
- Allocation-failure paths (C [38;5;10mmalloc[0m returning NULL vs. Rust [38;5;10mtry_reserve_exact[0m) cannot be triggered from a test.[0m[0m
- C's intermediate buffer is [38;5;10mmalloc[0m'd while Rust uses a [38;5;10mVec[0m; it is freed before return and never observable.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The signedness of C's [38;5;10mchar[0m was a real concern — bytes ≥ 0x80 are negative in C's comparisons but not in Rust's [38;5;10mu8[0m — but both fall through to the same result, and the exhaustive byte-pair and representative-quad tests (which include [38;5;10m0x80[0m and [38;5;10m0xff[0m) confirm it empirically.[0m[0m
